## Porcelain counts at capture

Compact `git status --porcelain` (directories collapsed):

| Status | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| Modified (`M`) | 158 |
| Deleted (`D`) | 10 |
| Untracked (`??`) | 42 |
| **Total porcelain records** | **210** |
| Generated sources in dirty set (`.generated.`) | 28 |

Expanded inventory uses `git status --porcelain -uall` so untracked package
trees (Harbor, SDK `src/http`, audit snapshot files) are one row per file.
After PSR-0a artifact creation the expanded total is recorded in
[`psr-0a-worktree-inventory.yaml`](./psr-0a-worktree-inventory.yaml) (`totals`).

## Recovery rules

Use these only to restore a **single path** or to re-read baseline truth. Do not
treat them as a bulk cleanup procedure.

```bash
# Re-read committed content of a modified/deleted tracked path
git show 30aa98e8df1d34aa1d372ad1e0e1a14750ec63cf:<path>

# Restore one tracked path to HEAD (destroys uncommitted edits on that path)
git checkout 30aa98e8df1d34aa1d372ad1e0e1a14750ec63cf -- <path>

# List dirty set as inventoried
git status --porcelain
```

Untracked paths are **not** recoverable from `HEAD`. Their `content_sha256` in
[`psr-0a-worktree-inventory.yaml`](./psr-0a-worktree-inventory.yaml) is the
recovery fingerprint for this snapshot moment only. Copy those files aside
before any deletion in later waves.

## Explicitly forbidden in PSR-0a / before inventory gate

- `git clean -fdX` or any recursive ignored-file wipe
- Hard reset of the branch (`git reset --hard`)
- Deleting `TEMP/`, `temp/`, or `docs/temp/` as a batch
- Combining all dirty paths into one “cleanup” commit
- Starting PSR-1 delete candidates before inventory exit gate is green

